  "answer_text": "Calcium is an essential mineral that your body needs to build and maintain strong bones and teeth. It also plays a vital role in blood clotting, muscle contraction, and nerve function. \n\nFor most adults, the Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is **1,000 mg of calcium per day**, which increases to **1,200 mg per day** for women over 50 and men over 70. \n\nHere is a breakdown of calcium-rich foods categorized by food type, including estimated calcium content per serving:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Dairy Products\nDairy is the most traditional and easily absorbed source of calcium.\n*   **Yogurt (Plain, low-fat):** ~415 mg per 8 oz (1 cup)\n*   **Yogurt (Greek):** ~200\u2013250 mg per 6 oz container\n*   **Milk (Whole, low-fat, or skim):** ~300\u2013325 mg per cup (8 oz)\n*   **Cheese:** \n    *   *Parmesan:* ~330 mg per oz (1 tbsp has about 55 mg)\n    *   *Romano/Swiss:* ~220\u2013240 mg per oz\n    *   *Cheddar/Mozzarella:* ~200 mg per oz\n\n### 2. Seafood (with soft bones)\nTiny, soft-boned fish are exceptionally high in calcium because you eat the bones.\n*   **Canned Sardines (with bones, in oil):** ~325 mg per 3 oz\n*   **Canned Pink Salmon (with bones):** ~180 mg per 3 oz\n\n### 3. Plant-Based and Fortified Foods\nFor those who are lactose intolerant, vegan, or simply prefer plant-based diets, fortified foods and soy-based products are excellent options.\n*   **Fortified Plant Milks (Soy, Almond, Oat):** ~300\u2013450 mg per cup (often fortified to match or exceed dairy milk)\n*   **Tofu (prepared with calcium sulfate/calcium-set):** ~200\u2013500 mg per \u00bd cup\n*   **Fortified Orange Juice:** ~350 mg per cup\n*   **Fortified Breakfast Cereals:** up to 1,000 mg per serving (always check the nutrition facts label)\n*   **Tempeh:** ~110 mg per \u00bd cup\n\n### 4. Leafy Greens & Vegetables\nMany green vegetables are great sources of calcium, though some (like spinach) contain oxalates, which can slightly reduce how much calcium your body actually absorbs.\n*   **Collard Greens (cooked):** ~268 mg per cup\n*   **Spinach (cooked):** ~245 mg per cup (high calcium, but lower absorption rate)\n*   **Kale (cooked):** ~179 mg per cup\n*   **Bok Choy / Pak Choi (cooked):** ~160 mg per cup\n*   **Broccoli (cooked):** ~60\u2013100 mg per cup\n\n### 5. Seeds, Nuts, and Legumes\nThese make for healthy, calcium-dense snacks or toppings.\n*   **Sesame Seeds:** ~280 mg per 1 oz (about 1 tbsp of tahini has ~64 mg)\n*   **Chia Seeds:** ~179 mg per 1 oz (about 2 tbsp)\n*   **Almonds:** ~75\u201380 mg per 1 oz (about 23 almonds)\n*   **White Beans (cooked):** ~130 mg per cup\n*   **Edamame (soybeans, cooked):** ~98 mg per cup\n\n---\n\n### Tips for Better Calcium Absorption\n*   **Don't forget Vitamin D:** Your body cannot properly absorb calcium without Vitamin D. Make sure you are getting enough sunlight, or eat foods rich in Vitamin D like egg yolks, fatty fish, and fortified milks.\n*   **Spread it out:** Your body absorbs calcium best in doses of **500 mg or less** at one time. Try to eat calcium-rich foods throughout the day rather than all at once.\n*   **How to read food labels:** On nutrition labels in the US, calcium is listed as a percentage of the Daily Value (% DV). A quick trick to find the exact milligrams is to look at the % DV, drop the percentage sign, and add a zero (e.g., 20% DV = 200 mg of calcium).",
